1. Create monitoring namespace
kubectl create namespace monitoring

Check:

kubectl get ns
2. Install Helm repository
helm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update

Verify:

helm repo list
3. Configure Kubernetes storage

Your kubeadm cluster initially had:

kubectl get sc

No resources found

This caused:

Install ebs-csi driver
kubectl apply -k "github.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-ebs-csi-driver/deploy/kubernetes/overlays/stable/?ref=master"


Make sure th ec2 has iam role accordingly 
pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims

For AWS EC2 kubeadm, install/configure the AWS EBS CSI driver and create a gp3 StorageClass.

Verify:

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep ebs

You should have the controller and node components running.

Then:

kubectl get sc

Expected:

NAME            PROVISIONER       VOLUMEBINDINGMODE
gp3 (default)   ebs.csi.aws.com   WaitForFirstConsumer
4. Test EBS before installing Loki

This is a very useful step.

Create a test PVC:

apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: ebs-test
  namespace: monitoring
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: gp3
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi

Apply:

kubectl apply -f ebs-test.yaml

Check:

kubectl get pvc -n monitoring

Expected:

ebs-test   Bound

Once this works, Loki's PVC can use the same storage class.

5. Loki configuration

Your initial Loki installation failed because scalable/distributed Loki requires object storage.

The error was:

Cannot run scalable targets (backend, read, write)
or distributed targets without an object storage backend.

For a small kubeadm lab, use Loki's monolithic/single-binary mode rather than distributed mode.

Your working values.yaml should be based on this approach:

deploymentMode: SingleBinary

loki:
  auth_enabled: false

  commonConfig:
    replication_factor: 1

  storage:
    type: filesystem

  schemaConfig:
    configs:
      - from: "2024-04-01"
        store: tsdb
        object_store: filesystem
        schema: v13
        index:
          prefix: index_
          period: 24h

singleBinary:
  replicas: 1

  persistence:
    enabled: true
    storageClass: gp3
    size: 10Gi

gateway:
  enabled: false

backend:
  replicas: 0

read:
  replicas: 0

write:
  replicas: 0

chunksCache:
  enabled: false

resultsCache:
  enabled: false

lokiCanary:
  enabled: true

test:
  enabled: false

Then install:

helm install loki grafana/loki \
  -n monitoring \
  -f values.yaml

Check:

kubectl get pods -n monitoring

Expected:

loki-0   2/2   Running

Check PVC:

kubectl get pvc -n monitoring

Expected:

storage-loki-0   Bound
6. Verify Loki

Check service:

kubectl get svc -n monitoring

You should have:

loki   ClusterIP   ...   3100/TCP

Test Loki:

kubectl port-forward svc/loki 3100:3100 -n monitoring

In another terminal:

curl http://localhost:3100/ready

Expected:

ready

You can also check:

curl http://localhost:3100/metrics
7. Install Promtail

Promtail is deployed as a DaemonSet, so each Kubernetes node gets a Promtail pod.

Create:

vi promtail-values.yaml

Use:

config:
  clients:
    - url: http://loki.monitoring.svc.cluster.local:3100/loki/api/v1/push

  snippets:
    pipelineStages:
      - cri: {}

    scrapeConfigs: |
      - job_name: kubernetes-pods

        kubernetes_sd_configs:
          - role: pod

        relabel_configs:

          - source_labels:
              - __meta_kubernetes_namespace
            target_label: namespace

          - source_labels:
              - __meta_kubernetes_pod_name
            target_label: pod

          - source_labels:
              - __meta_kubernetes_pod_container_name
            target_label: container

          - source_labels:
              - __meta_kubernetes_pod_node_name
            target_label: node_name

          - action: replace
            source_labels:
              - __meta_kubernetes_pod_uid
              - __meta_kubernetes_pod_container_name
            separator: /
            regex: (.*)/(.*)
            replacement: /var/log/pods/*$1/$2/*.log
            target_label: __path__
Important

Do not add:

extraVolumes:

or:

extraVolumeMounts:

The Promtail chart already mounts the required Kubernetes log directories.

Install:

helm install promtail grafana/promtail \
  -n monitoring \
  -f promtail-values.yaml
8. Verify Promtail
kubectl get pods -n monitoring -o wide

Expected:

promtail-xxxxx   1/1   Running

For multiple Kubernetes nodes:

promtail-xxxxx   1/1   Running   k8s-master
promtail-yyyyy   1/1   Running   k8s-worker01

Check logs:

kubectl logs daemonset/promtail -n monitoring --tail=100

You want to see:

tail routine: started
path=/var/log/pods/...

This confirms Promtail is actually reading Kubernetes logs.

9. Important Promtail error we fixed

Initially you had:

no path for target

This means:

Promtail discovered pod
        ↓
but didn't know which log file to read

The important configuration that fixed it was:

- action: replace
  source_labels:
    - __meta_kubernetes_pod_uid
    - __meta_kubernetes_pod_container_name
  separator: /
  regex: (.*)/(.*)
  replacement: /var/log/pods/*$1/$2/*.log
  target_label: __path__

After that:

promtail   1/1 Running

and:

tail routine: started

appeared.

10. Verify Loki is receiving logs

Port-forward:

kubectl port-forward svc/loki 3100:3100 -n monitoring

Then:

curl http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/labels

You should get labels similar to:

{
  "status": "success",
  "data": [
    "container",
    "namespace",
    "node_name",
    "pod"
  ]
}

This proves:

Promtail → Loki

is working.

11. Install Grafana

Install:

helm install grafana grafana/grafana \
  -n monitoring \
  --set adminPassword=admin123 \
  --set service.type=NodePort

Check:

kubectl get svc grafana -n monitoring

Example:

grafana   NodePort   80:32000/TCP

Get your node public IP:

kubectl get nodes -o wide

